<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Bead nexus-vsipz (RDR-213 engine half, Sam's decision 2026-09-18: "land
    the cursor now, engine fix on the next cut" — T2 nexus_rdr/213-decision-
    announcements-rate-limited-not-ack-gated-2026-09-17; analysis
    nexus_rdr/213-waiter-deep-analysis-2026-09-17 (2/2) section D).

    THE DEFECT this column pair fixes: the client-side cursor
    (nexus-gomuo.1, cherry-picked onto this same tree) advances past every
    row it announces by (created_at, id). out() stamps created_at with
    Postgres now(), the TRANSACTION START time (TupleRepository.java
    writeOut, ~line 549). Transaction A starts at t1, transaction B starts
    at t2 > t1 and commits FIRST; a reader's cursor advances to B's
    (created_at, id); A commits afterward with created_at t1, strictly
    BEHIND the already-issued cursor, and is never returned to that reader.
    Measured 2026-09-17 (tests/test_mcp_channel.py::
    test_stop_rule_two_hundred_rows_two_concurrent_writers_no_row_ever_lost_to_the_cursor):
    2 failures in 9 runs under heavy concurrent box load.

    THE FIX: move the position that gates re-delivery from the CLIENT (a
    cursor over created_at/id, which a late commit can appear behind) to
    the ROW itself (announced_at/announce_count, stamped in the same
    statement that selects the row for announcement). A query that always
    re-scans the full claimable-and-due set, oldest first, cannot skip a
    row that commits late — there is no cursor position for it to land
    behind. TupleRepository.queryOnce's new announce-mode branch (bead
    nexus-vsipz Java half) is the consumer; docs/wire-contract-pending.md
    carries the wire-ledger entry.

    Two nullable/defaulted columns, no backfill:
      - announced_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL: NULL means "never announced" for
        every existing row (there is nothing to migrate — no prior column
        held this fact, so NULL is not a stand-in for a lost value, it is
        the true previous state of every row that predates this bead).
      - announce_count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0: 0 for every existing row,
        for the identical reason — no row has ever been announced under
        this mechanism, so 0 is not a computed backfill, it is the correct
        starting count.
    Both are ADD COLUMN only (no UPDATE, no ALTER ... TYPE ... USING) —
    scripts/data_effect_lint.py's own scope excludes ADD COLUMN from the
    DATA EFFECT disclosure requirement, and this changeset's comment says
    so explicitly per that module's convention, matching catalog-022-owner-
    soft-delete.xml's ADD COLUMN shape (also additive, also no DATA EFFECT
    line). DDL, not DML — nexus.tuples carries FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY
    (tuples-001-1), but ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN is unaffected by RLS (RLS
    gates row DATA access, not schema changes), so no toggle is needed here
    (contrast tuples-004-1's UPDATE, which does need one).

    Placed after tuples-005-subspace-and-claim-log-indexes.xml (current
    tail of the tuples-00N family) and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml
    runAlways block, matching the established late-append convention
    (D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <!-- ── Changeset 1: announced_at / announce_count on nexus.tuples ──────── -->
    <changeSet id="tuples-006-1" author="nexus-vsipz">
        <comment>Adds nexus.tuples.announced_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL and announce_count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 -- additive columns only (ADD COLUMN, no data mutation of any existing row's value), so no DATA EFFECT line applies per scripts/data_effect_lint.py's own scope. NULL/0 are the true prior state of every existing row (this mechanism has never run before), not a computed backfill. Consumed by TupleRepository's new announce-mode wait/rd query branch (bead nexus-vsipz): a mailbox WaitSpec carrying an announce field returns only claimable rows due for a first-or-repeat announcement and stamps both columns, in the same statement, on every row it returns.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.tuples
    ADD COLUMN announced_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL,
    ADD COLUMN announce_count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true">
                ALTER TABLE nexus.tuples DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS announced_at;
                ALTER TABLE nexus.tuples DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS announce_count;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
